{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST bundle a skill for deriving initial requirements from an existing codebase. The skill MUST direct agents to read the code and tests, map every first-party test to at least one requirement or example, identify and map every source unit implementing end-user-visible functionality or interactions to a requirement, and report gaps rather than claim complete coverage without an independent inventory review.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill` governs a bundled skill's text. Its only test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_is_bundled_and_directs_complete_inventories]`. It checks the shipped text of the skill, which is the right way to test a requirement about what a skill directs. I read `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md` to confirm the asserted phrases are present and mean what the requirement needs.\n\nThe helpers behave as intended. `bundled_skill` reads the skill's embedded files (`include_str!`, via `skill_files`) and whitespace-collapses them. `assert_phrases` fails if any phrase is missing. `assert_in_order` goes through `assert_text_in_order`, which fails unless each phrase appears after the previous one.\n\n| Obligation | Test assertion | Where the skill says it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Habeas bundles the skill. | `SKILLS.contains(\"habeas-bootstrap-requirements\")`, and the description must start with \"Use when adopting Habeas in an existing codebase\". | The directory list and publication are checked by the cited `requirements.cli.skill_structure` test, which is context and not reassessed here. |\n| Direct agents to read the code and tests. | Phrases \"Read the existing requirements, documentation, public entry points, implementation, and tests\" and \"Follow behavior through the code\". | Workflow step 2. |\n| Map every first-party test to a requirement or example. | Phrases \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"Record the behavior each test checks; fill in requirement names once they are installed\", \"Every test must end up with a `{test}` reference to a requirement or example\", and \"Give every inventoried test and each requirement's roots their references\". | The Inventories \"Tests\" bullet and the Tagging section. |\n| Identify and map every end-user-visible source unit to a requirement. | Phrases \"every function, method, struct, enum, and union, listed from the source to discover behavior no requirement states\", \"public APIs, commands, handlers, UI actions, configuration, outputs, errors, and distinct observable branches\", and \"A tagged entry point doesn't mean every observable branch beneath it has a requirement\". | The Inventories \"Items and behavior\" bullet. |\n| Report gaps and not claim complete coverage without an independent inventory review. | Ordered phrases: \"recount both inventories from the source and tests without your lists\", then the gap-list line `- <item or behavior> (<file>:<line>): <reason>; <open question>`, then \"Claim 100% only when both inventories have no unmapped items\", then \"Don't silently exclude difficult cases\". | Workflow step 8, the Report template, and the closing paragraph. |\n\nPlausible regressions the test would catch:\n- Dropping the test inventory or the source-unit inventory.\n- Dropping the observable-branch caveat.\n- Removing the independent recount.\n- Removing the gap-list format.\n- Allowing a 100% claim without the recount.\n- Reordering the recount, the report and the claim rule.\n\nOne thing the test does not assert is the sentence \"Map each other inventoried item to the requirement whose root reaches it.\" It does assert the inventory of every source unit, the rule that roots and tests get their references, and the observable-branch caveat. Together these cover the obligation to map end-user-visible units to requirements. I treat the remaining sentence as elaboration and not a separate obligation.\n\nOther tests mention the bootstrap skill, for example the references and coverage tests. They serve other requirements and are not needed here.\n\nI found no adequacy gap, and no `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "a41a2920cceb608b61a78120cb3754621a2f77f6e7943141d0ef70b81bece077",
